-- 2026-08-22 -- fix orders.order_value_usd / order_value_inr (and
-- order_value_original where it was never entered) against the user's
-- offline reconciliation file (invoice_details.xlsx, 655 rows), per the
-- user's explicit "mera ofline data sahi hai" (my offline data is correct).
--
-- WHAT WAS FOUND (auto-generated VALUES-table diff from the source xlsx,
-- not hand-typed -- same rigorous method as the 8-vendor-ledger round):
--
-- Of the file's 655 rows:
--  - 64 have no matching order in `orders` at all (old numeric-style ref_nos
--    like PO-302, PO-1001..PO-1009, PO-425, PO-475, PO-480, etc.) -- these
--    are NOT a data-value bug, there is no order row to update. Confirmed
--    via ILIKE search that no near-variant ref_no exists either. Almost
--    certainly the same class of pre-`orders`-table legacy record already
--    documented in the P&L-live round (order_id IS NULL rows in
--    sale_profit_ledger, predating this system). NOT fixed by this
--    migration -- flagged back to the user, not guessed at further.
--  - 591 matched an order by ref_no. Of those:
--      - 55 (ALL created_at = 2026-08-08, a single historical bulk-import
--        batch spanning order_date 2026-04-03 to 2026-07-09, 3 companies)
--        have order_value_original = order_value_usd = order_value_inr =
--        0.00 -- the sale amount was simply never entered for this batch.
--      - 535 have a correct order_value_usd but an order_value_inr ~3-4%
--        HIGHER than the file, traced to a missing Exchange Rate Master
--        entry for their date range: computeCurrencyConversion() fell back
--        to a single flat "Live market estimate" rate (95.2100) applied to
--        every order in the batch regardless of its actual date, instead of
--        the correct per-date historical rate (~91-92 in the file).
--      - 1 (PO-A222-3/3) has a trivial $0.99 USD difference (415.01 in DB
--        vs 416.00 in the file) -- corrected by the same blanket fix below,
--        not worth a special case.
--
-- FIX: for every matched USD order, set order_value_original/usd/inr
-- directly from the file (this single UPDATE naturally covers all three
-- categories above -- the zero-value batch, the pure-INR-rate mismatches,
-- and the one small USD typo). order_value_original = order_value_usd for
-- every USD-currency order per computeCurrencyConversion()'s own logic
-- (src/lib/orders/currency.ts: "usd = round2(originalValue)" -- no
-- conversion happens for the USD leg), so it's safe to set both from the
-- same expected_usd figure. Restricted to order_currency = 'USD' as a
-- defensive guard (every matched row was confirmed USD, but this ensures
-- the UPDATE can never silently touch a non-USD order that happens to
-- share a ref_no). Restricted further to only rows that actually differ,
-- so this is a true idempotent "fix mismatches" migration, not a blanket
-- overwrite.
--
-- exchange_rate_source is updated to record that this was a manual
-- reconciliation-file correction, not a recomputation, so a future session
-- doesn't mistake it for a live/official-rate value.
--
-- This does NOT touch the Exchange Rate Master table -- backfilling correct
-- historical rates for 2026-04 through 2026-07 (so this class of error
-- can't recur for any order that gets edited/recomputed later) is a
-- separate, still-open follow-up, not part of this migration.
--
-- Dry-run tested against local scratch Postgres before delivery.

